WEBVTT

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:08.119
Cool, so we've hit the ground running now.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.748
We have our running
animation setup, we have

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.480
a jumping animation, so we
can fly through the air so excited.

00:00:15.481 --> 00:00:19.360
Now we just need to add one more thing, which
is whenever we collide with one of these

00:00:19.361 --> 00:00:23.240
barriers, we're not just going to
keep running into one of the barriers.

00:00:23.241 --> 00:00:29.640
So what we can do is we can use one
of our other layers for character deaths.

00:00:29.641 --> 00:00:34.640
Now in our deaths layer, you can see all of
the different kinds of states that they can

00:00:34.641 --> 00:00:38.392
go through, so we can
go from alive to a death

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:44.668
one, so let's see if we double click that,
and we'll see we'll play that, see how that

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.600
looks, falls over, and
then death one is just

00:00:47.601 --> 00:00:54.600
the character falling over, death
two, what does this one look like?

00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:58.698
falls over in front, so falling
over front doesn't really make

00:00:58.699 --> 00:01:04.239
any sense in this case, so we'll
probably just go with death one.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:10.680
Now let's see how we can actually make
this death animation play when it happens.

00:01:10.681 --> 00:01:14.552
So let's see the
transitions here, so let's see

00:01:14.564 --> 00:01:18.200
the conditions are, so
the death bull should

00:01:18.201 --> 00:01:25.280
be set to true, and there's a death
type integer that's equal to one.

00:01:25.281 --> 00:01:29.054
So as a good way to
kind of manage the state,

00:01:29.066 --> 00:01:32.600
we can actually see
the second transition,

00:01:32.601 --> 00:01:35.960
and you can see death type
becomes equal to two in that case.

00:01:36.559 --> 00:01:41.080
So since we want the first death animation,
that's the one that we'll go with.

00:01:41.081 --> 00:01:45.073
So if we go into our scripts
again, all the way down

00:01:45.173 --> 00:01:49.480
at the bottom here, go to
our player controller script.

00:01:49.481 --> 00:01:53.640
So now in our player controller script,
we're going to go to the place where we end

00:01:53.641 --> 00:02:00.280
the game, where we have
that game over, bit of code.

00:02:00.281 --> 00:02:03.569
So down in our on
collision enter, you can

00:02:03.581 --> 00:02:08.039
see if we collide with an
obstacle, then the game is over.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.347
So this seems like a
pretty good place where

00:02:10.359 --> 00:02:15.360
we can actually make
this death animation occur.

00:02:16.275 --> 00:02:24.200
So let's see what kind of methods
we have for our player animator.

00:02:24.201 --> 00:02:25.201
So let's see.

00:02:25.275 --> 00:02:29.000
So we had set trigger to set a trigger.

00:02:29.001 --> 00:02:33.520
Now that death b was a
bull, so can we set bull?

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.080
Oh, cool, perfect.

00:02:37.505 --> 00:02:45.080
So we'll set bull, and we wanted
the death bull to be true, so let's see.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.320
We can use an int id or
we can use the string name.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:57.160
So I believe the name was
capital d f underscore b for the bull.

00:02:57.161 --> 00:03:04.480
And then the value that we want to set
it to, so in this case, we set it to true.

00:03:04.481 --> 00:03:05.481
Cool.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:07.569
So now we set death
to true, but we need to

00:03:07.581 --> 00:03:11.120
actually make sure that we
select the proper death type.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:23.200
So let's see if we can actually set that
to, so player anim, and then let's see.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:32.640
So it was an integer, so there's a set
integer option to, so we'll select that one.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:41.200
And I believe to probably use a string
again, just double check, string name.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:48.995
So then it was, believe
it was a capital d f

00:03:49.007 --> 00:03:52.640
capital t type, and
then an underscore int.

00:03:52.641 --> 00:03:58.440
And then I believe the one that
we wanted was the first one, so one.

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:01.746
So now if we save,
so hopefully if we typed

00:04:01.758 --> 00:04:04.829
everything properly,
and we set the proper

00:04:04.904 --> 00:04:10.240
values, it should, when
we go into our game, cool.

00:04:10.241 --> 00:04:13.161
So when we play, let's
see what happens when

00:04:13.173 --> 00:04:17.290
we run into the obstacle,
wait for one of them to spawn.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.774
Oh, there they go,
and falls over, and then

00:04:20.786 --> 00:04:24.080
it goes into the dead
state in our animation.

00:04:24.081 --> 00:04:27.200
So perfect.

00:04:27.201 --> 00:04:30.551
So now what you're
going to do to add in this

00:04:30.563 --> 00:04:34.000
animation is in your
player controller script,

00:04:34.001 --> 00:04:37.476
in our on collision
enter method, where we

00:04:37.488 --> 00:04:41.640
actually collide with an
obstacle to end our game.

00:04:41.641 --> 00:04:46.160
That's where we're going to
use our player animator variable.

00:04:46.161 --> 00:04:52.280
And we're going to set the
animation to be our death be.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:55.076
And since our death
be is a Boolean, we use

00:04:55.088 --> 00:04:58.040
set bool to set it to
true for our death type,

00:04:58.165 --> 00:05:02.920
because it's an integer, we set it with
integer, and we'll set that one to one.

00:05:02.921 --> 00:05:05.978
So then when we go
play the game, you can

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:09.360
watch as the animator
window transitions when

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:18.800
the player hits one of the obstacles.